// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Physics C uses calculus to describe motion exactly, even when acceleration isn't constant.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"v(t) = dx/dt and a(t) = dv/dt. Going backward: x(t) = x₀ + ∫ v dt and v(t) = v₀ + ∫ a dt."},{"t":"p","text":"The constant-acceleration equations are special cases. When acceleration changes with time, integrate."},{"t":"ex","problem":"a(t) = 6t m/s² and v(0) = 2 m/s. Find v(t) and v(3).","steps":["v(t) = 2 + ∫₀ᵗ 6t dt = 2 + 3t²"],"answer":"v(3) = 29 m/s"},{"t":"p","text":"Displacement is the integral of velocity; distance traveled is the integral of speed |v|. On graphs, slope and area still give derivatives and integrals."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Rocket sled with increasing thrust","aim":"A sled's acceleration grows steadily with time (a = kt). Use calculus to predict its velocity and position.","sets":[{"name":"t","start":3,"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1},{"name":"kk","start":2,"label":"Jerk k (m/s³)","min":0.5,"max":5,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"a","expr":"kk*t","label":"Acceleration (m/s²)"},{"name":"v","expr":"kk*t^2/2","label":"Velocity (m/s)"},{"name":"x","expr":"kk*t^3/6","label":"Position (m)"}],"steps":["Record velocity and position every 2 seconds.","Check that each column is the integral of the one before."],"noise":2,"result":"Integrating a = kt gives v = kt²/2 and x = kt³/6. Velocity is the area under the a–t graph, and position is the area under the v–t graph.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"v"}}]},{"say":"In two dimensions, treat each direction separately and use vectors to combine them.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Position, velocity and acceleration are vectors: r(t) = x(t)î + y(t)ĵ, v = dr/dt, a = dv/dt."},{"t":"p","text":"For projectiles, aₓ = 0 and a_y = −g. The horizontal and vertical motions are independent but share the same time."},{"t":"p","text":"Relative velocity: v_A relative to C = v_A relative to B + v_B relative to C."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A ball is launched at 20 m/s at 30° above horizontal from level ground. Find its time of flight.","steps":["v_y0 = 20 sin 30° = 10 m/s","Lands when y = 0: t = 2v_y0/g = 20/9.8"],"answer":"about 2.04 s"}]}],[{"say":"Newton's second law becomes a differential equation when forces depend on velocity. That's Physics C's specialty.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"ΣF = ma = m dv/dt. For systems, the center of mass accelerates according to the net external force."},{"t":"p","text":"Friction: f_k = μ_kN; static friction up to μ_sN."},{"t":"p","text":"Resistive (drag) forces like F = −bv lead to differential equations: m dv/dt = mg − bv. The object approaches terminal velocity v_T = mg/b, with v(t) = v_T(1 − e^(−bt/m))."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 2 kg ball falls with drag F = −0.5v. What is its terminal velocity?","steps":["At terminal velocity, mg = bv","v_T = (2 × 9.8)/0.5"],"answer":"39.2 m/s"},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Falling coffee filters","aim":"Drop stacks of coffee filters and track their speed as air drag builds up (drag ∝ v²).","sets":[{"name":"t","start":0.5,"label":"Time after release (s)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.05},{"name":"n","start":1,"label":"Filters stacked","min":1,"max":8,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"vT","expr":"sqrt(n*0.0009*g/0.0016)","label":"Terminal speed (m/s)"},{"name":"v","expr":"vT*tanh(g*t/vT)","label":"Speed (m/s)"}],"steps":["Record the speed every 0.5 s for one filter.","Repeat with 4 filters and compare terminal speeds."],"noise":3,"result":"Speed rises, then levels off at terminal velocity where drag equals weight. With quadratic drag, terminal speed is proportional to √m: four filters fall only twice as fast as one.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"v"}}]},{"say":"Circular motion and gravity combine in orbits. Calculus lets us handle them precisely.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Centripetal acceleration a_c = v²/r = ω²r toward the center. If speed changes, there's also tangential acceleration dv/dt."},{"t":"p","text":"Universal gravitation: F = Gm₁m₂/r². Gravitational field g = GM/r². Gravitational potential energy U = −Gm₁m₂/r (zero at infinity)."},{"t":"p","text":"Orbits: for circular orbits, GMm/r² = mv²/r, so v = √(GM/r). Escape speed: v_esc = √(2GM/R). Kepler's third law: T² ∝ r³."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Why is gravitational potential energy U = −GMm/r negative?","steps":["U = −GMm/r is zero at infinity and decreases as objects get closer"],"answer":"Bound objects have negative total energy; escaping requires raising total energy to zero"}]}],[{"say":"With calculus, work is an integral, and force is the negative slope of potential energy.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"W = ∫ F · dx. For a spring, W = ∫ kx dx = ½kx². The work–energy theorem: W_net = ΔK."},{"t":"p","text":"For conservative forces, F = −dU/dx. Equilibrium points occur where dU/dx = 0: stable at minima of U, unstable at maxima."},{"t":"ex","problem":"F(x) = 3x² N acts from x = 0 to 2 m. Find the work done.","steps":["W = ∫₀² 3x² dx = x³ from 0 to 2"],"answer":"8 J"},{"t":"p","text":"Conservative forces (gravity, springs) have path-independent work; nonconservative forces (friction) dissipate mechanical energy."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Stretching a nonlinear spring","aim":"Stretch a rubber cord with a force probe. Its force isn't linear, so find the work done by integrating.","sets":[{"name":"x","start":0.1,"label":"Stretch (m)","min":0,"max":0.3,"step":0.01},{"name":"kk","start":100,"label":"Linear constant k (N/m)","min":20,"max":200,"step":10},{"name":"b","start":2000,"label":"Cubic constant β (N/m³)","min":0,"max":5000,"step":100}],"outs":[{"name":"F","expr":"kk*x + b*x^3","label":"Force (N)"},{"name":"W","expr":"kk*x^2/2 + b*x^4/4","label":"Work done (J)"}],"steps":["Record the force and work for 5 stretches.","Set β to 0 to compare with an ideal spring."],"noise":2,"result":"Work is the area under the F–x curve: W = ∫F dx = ½kx² + ¼βx⁴. With β = 0 it's the familiar ½kx²; the stiffening term adds more work at large stretches.","plot":{"x":"x","y":"F"}}]},{"say":"Energy conservation solves problems that would be messy with forces alone.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"K₁ + U₁ + W_other = K₂ + U₂. With only conservative forces, mechanical energy is constant."},{"t":"p","text":"Power P = dW/dt = F · v. Average power = W/Δt."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 1,500 kg car moves at 20 m/s against a total resistive force of 600 N. What power must the engine supply to keep speed constant?","steps":["At constant speed, engine force = 600 N","P = Fv = 600 × 20"],"answer":"12,000 W (12 kW)"},{"t":"p","text":"Energy diagrams (U vs. x with a total energy line) show turning points where K = 0 and regions where motion is allowed."}]}],[{"say":"Momentum and impulse handle collisions and changing forces with ease.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"p = mv. Impulse J = ∫ F dt = Δp. ΣF = dp/dt."},{"t":"p","text":"On a force–time graph, the area under the curve is the impulse."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A force F(t) = 10t N acts on a 2 kg object from t = 0 to 3 s. Find the change in velocity.","steps":["J = ∫₀³ 10t dt = 5t² from 0 to 3 = 45 N·s"],"answer":"Δv = 45/2 = 22.5 m/s"},{"t":"p","text":"Center of mass: x_cm = Σmᵢxᵢ/Σmᵢ, or ∫x dm / M for continuous objects. If no net external force acts, the center of mass moves at constant velocity."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Force sensor collision","aim":"Crash a 0.5 kg cart into a force sensor with different bumpers and measure the impulse.","sets":[{"name":"dt","start":20,"label":"Collision time (ms)","min":5,"max":100,"step":5},{"name":"F","start":100,"label":"Peak force (N)","min":10,"max":500,"step":10}],"outs":[{"name":"J","expr":"2*F*dt/1000/pi","label":"Impulse (N·s)"},{"name":"dv","expr":"J/0.5","label":"Change in velocity (m/s)"}],"steps":["Record the impulse for 4 collision times at the same peak force.","Now keep the impulse about 1 N·s and see how the peak force must change with collision time."],"noise":3,"result":"Impulse is the area under the force–time curve (∫F dt = Δp). For the same change in momentum, a longer collision means a smaller peak force, which is how airbags and crumple zones protect people.","plot":{"x":"dt","y":"J"}}]},{"say":"In collisions, momentum is always conserved. Kinetic energy depends on the type of collision.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Isolated system: Σp_before = Σp_after. Elastic collisions also conserve kinetic energy; inelastic do not. Perfectly inelastic collisions end with the objects stuck together."},{"t":"p","text":"For a 1D elastic collision between equal masses where one is at rest, they exchange velocities."},{"t":"p","text":"In 2D, conserve momentum separately in the x and y directions."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 3 kg cart at 4 m/s sticks to a 1 kg cart at rest. Find the final speed and kinetic energy lost.","steps":["12 = 4v, so v = 3 m/s","K before = 24 J; K after = ½(4)(9) = 18 J"],"answer":"v = 3 m/s; 6 J lost"}]}],[{"say":"Rotation has its own versions of position, velocity and mass. Calculus computes rotational inertia for any shape.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"ω = dθ/dt, α = dω/dt. Linking to linear: v = rω, a_t = rα. Moment of inertia I = Σmr² = ∫r² dm."},{"t":"p","text":"Common results: thin rod about its center ML²/12, about one end ML²/3; solid disk ½MR²; hoop MR²; solid sphere (2/5)MR²."},{"t":"p","text":"Parallel axis theorem: I = I_cm + Md²."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Use the parallel axis theorem to find I for a rod about its end from I_cm = ML²/12.","steps":["d = L/2","ML²/12 + M(L/2)² = ML²/12 + 3ML²/12"],"answer":"ML²/3"}]},{"say":"Torque causes angular acceleration, just as force causes linear acceleration.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"τ = r × F, with magnitude rF sin θ. Στ = Iα. For static equilibrium: ΣF = 0 and Στ = 0 about any point."},{"t":"p","text":"Systems with ropes over pulleys with mass combine translational and rotational equations, linked by a = rα."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 2 kg disk (R = 0.5 m) has a string wrapped around it pulled with 4 N. Find α.","steps":["I = ½(2)(0.5²) = 0.25 kg·m²","τ = 4 × 0.5 = 2 N·m"],"answer":"α = 2/0.25 = 8 rad/s²"},{"t":"tip","text":"For equilibrium problems, choose a pivot point where unknown forces act, so their torques are zero."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Torque on a pivoted rod","aim":"Hang a force probe on a rod at different angles and distances from the pivot and measure the torque.","sets":[{"name":"th","start":90,"label":"Angle between rod and force (°)","min":0,"max":180,"step":5},{"name":"r","start":0.3,"label":"Distance from pivot (m)","min":0.05,"max":1,"step":0.05},{"name":"F","start":10,"label":"Force (N)","min":1,"max":50,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"tau","expr":"r*F*sind(th)","label":"Torque (N·m)"}],"steps":["Record the torque for at least 5 angles.","Then change the distance from the pivot."],"noise":2,"result":"τ = rF sin θ is greatest when the force is perpendicular to the rod and zero when it points along it. Pushing farther from the hinge gives more torque, which is why door handles are on the far edge.","plot":{"x":"th","y":"tau"}}]}],[{"say":"Rotating objects store energy, and rolling objects have both translational and rotational energy.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"K_rot = ½Iω². Rolling without slipping: v_cm = Rω, K = ½Mv² + ½Iω². Work by torque: W = ∫τ dθ. Power: P = τω."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A solid sphere rolls without slipping down a ramp of height h. Find its speed at the bottom.","steps":["Mgh = ½Mv² + ½(2/5)MR²(v/R)² = (7/10)Mv²"],"answer":"v = √(10gh/7)"},{"t":"p","text":"Objects with smaller I/(MR²) roll faster down ramps: sphere beats disk beats hoop."},{"t":"oops","text":"Don't forget the rotational term. A rolling ball moves slower at the bottom of a ramp than a sliding block, because some energy goes into spinning."}]},{"say":"Angular momentum is conserved when no external torque acts, from spinning skaters to collapsing stars.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"L = Iω for rigid bodies; for a particle, L = r × p. Στ = dL/dt. If net external torque is zero, L is conserved."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 0.5 kg ball moving at 4 m/s sticks to the end of a 1 m rod (I = 0.2 kg·m²) pivoted at the other end. Find ω afterward.","steps":["Before: L = mvr = 0.5 × 4 × 1 = 2 kg·m²/s","After: I_total = 0.2 + 0.5(1²) = 0.7"],"answer":"ω = 2/0.7 ≈ 2.86 rad/s"},{"t":"p","text":"Angular momentum is conserved in rotational collisions, but kinetic energy usually is not."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Spinning stool with dumbbells","aim":"Spin on a stool holding dumbbells at arm's length (1 rad/s), then pull them in. How fast do you spin?","sets":[{"name":"r","start":0.9,"label":"Dumbbell distance from axis (m)","min":0.1,"max":0.9,"step":0.05},{"name":"m","start":3,"label":"Each dumbbell's mass (kg)","min":1,"max":5,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"I","expr":"2 + 2*m*r^2","label":"Rotational inertia (kg·m²)"},{"name":"w","expr":"(2 + 2*m*0.81)*1/I","label":"Angular speed (rad/s)"},{"name":"KE","expr":"0.5*I*w^2","label":"Rotational KE (J)"}],"steps":["Record the angular speed for 5 dumbbell positions.","Look at the kinetic energy column. Where does the extra energy come from?"],"noise":2,"result":"With no external torque, angular momentum Iω is conserved, so pulling the weights in lowers I and raises ω. Kinetic energy increases because your arms do work pulling the weights inward.","plot":{"x":"r","y":"w"}}]}],[{"say":"Simple harmonic motion is described by a differential equation whose solutions are sines and cosines.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"SHM occurs when F = −kx, giving d²x/dt² = −(k/m)x. Solution: x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) with ω = √(k/m)."},{"t":"p","text":"Period: mass-spring T = 2π√(m/k); simple pendulum T = 2π√(L/g) (small angles); physical pendulum T = 2π√(I/(mgd))."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Show that x(t) = A cos(ωt) satisfies the SHM equation.","steps":["d²x/dt² = −Aω² cos(ωt) = −ω²x"],"answer":"It works when ω² = k/m"},{"t":"p","text":"Velocity and acceleration: v_max = Aω at equilibrium; a_max = Aω² at the turning points."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Pendulum at large angles","aim":"Time a 1 m pendulum released from bigger and bigger angles. When does the small-angle formula fail?","sets":[{"name":"A","start":10,"label":"Release angle (°)","min":5,"max":90,"step":5},{"name":"L","start":1,"label":"Length (m)","min":0.2,"max":2,"step":0.1}],"outs":[{"name":"T0","expr":"2*pi*sqrt(L/g)","label":"Small-angle period (s)"},{"name":"T","expr":"T0*(1 + (A*pi/180)^2/16 + 11*(A*pi/180)^4/3072)","label":"Measured period (s)"},{"name":"err","expr":"100*(T - T0)/T0","label":"Difference (%)"}],"steps":["Record the period for at least 5 release angles."],"noise":0.5,"result":"For small angles the period is 2π√(L/g) whatever the amplitude, because sin θ ≈ θ. Above about 20° the period grows noticeably (7% longer at 60°), since the motion is no longer simple harmonic.","plot":{"x":"A","y":"T"},"sim":{"scene":"pendulum","map":{"L":"L","a":"A"}}}]},{"say":"Energy sloshes between kinetic and potential in an oscillator, while the total stays fixed.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"E = ½kA² = ½mv² + ½kx². So v = ω√(A² − x²)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A 0.25 kg mass on a spring (k = 100 N/m) oscillates with A = 0.2 m. Find its speed at x = 0.1 m.","steps":["ω = √(100/0.25) = 20 rad/s","v = 20√(0.04 − 0.01) = 20√0.03"],"answer":"about 3.46 m/s"},{"t":"p","text":"Damping removes energy, decreasing amplitude over time. Driving an oscillator at its natural frequency causes resonance, with large amplitudes."}]}]];
export default text;
